#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134684} :activity/id #uuid "52c145b5-305d-5e94-a854-8ca571ca1a03" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578 :topic/id #uuid "5558fd2c-f6e0-5362-bf8b-7d3500df79a0" :topic/math-academy-id 2127} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135054 :tutorial/content "The **Gram-Schmidt process** is an algorithm that we can use to construct an *orthogonal* basis if we are given a non-orthogonal basis to start with.\n\nFor example, consider the basis $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ for the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can use the above basis to construct an *orthogonal* basis for the given $2$-dimensional subspace. To do this, we use the Gram-Schmidt process, which involves $2$ steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Set ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}=\\mathbf{a}_1.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ in $V$ that's orthogonal to $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1.$ This can be achieved by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/67/678cc056e3c9d3fbf17423ac591b3e85d84ee8060c0637840fe1c4c9e87aa878.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1})$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So this means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\perp {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}.$\nMoreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nAfter applying the Gram-Schmidt process, we get the set $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}\\},$ which is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nSo, in our example, we start by setting $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ Then, we find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ that's orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b833ab7-f6d4-5c93-8709-37ecf29bf023" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3631 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e23dc2ed-aace-586d-8f71-e7621bc6d386" :step/math-academy-id 20257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133602 :question/id #uuid "c430a11a-8595-568f-9768-aad8a8de415f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5581"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049811}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354524}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "7dca9c20-2451-5bcc-8034-904527090719" :step/math-academy-id 20258 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133603 :question/id #uuid "47fadc87-f265-5f1c-827a-28e5be9e8121" :question/math-academy-id "e-5582"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366525}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "09154627-fce0-54d9-b9c1-232fc06abaf2" :step/math-academy-id 20259 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135055 :tutorial/content "Given a basis $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ for a subspace $V,$ we can use the Gram-Schmidt process to find an *orthonormal* basis of the subspace.\n\nTo do this, we simply execute the Gram-Schmidt process as before to obtain an orthogonal basis $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2\\},$ and then we normalize each vector in this basis to get the orthonormal basis $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2\\},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_1 \\Vert},\n\t\\quad\n\t\\mathbf{u}_2 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_2 \\Vert}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ea8b19b-f7a8-5339-abd4-3069e18b2555" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3632 :tutorial/title "Using the Gram-Schmidt Process to Find an Orthonormal Basis"} :step/id #uuid "881045a6-4243-5fbd-8617-d6520ecad604" :step/math-academy-id 20260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133604 :question/id #uuid "60dbc829-01fc-5283-8741-68af483d5313" :question/math-academy-id "e-5583"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366386}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "5c0057df-8020-54b5-b1ba-1011db749edc" :step/math-academy-id 20261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135089 :tutorial/content "Sometimes it's helpful to \"clean up\" the result of a Gram-Schmidt process by scaling the resulting vectors.\n\nFor example, suppose that the set $\\mathcal B,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}.$\n\nSince the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2,$ the vector $3\\mathbf{v}_2$ also lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp 3\\mathbf{v}_2.$ Therefore, taking \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ninstead of $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ we again obtain an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBut this time, the second vector in the basis looks slightly nicer and is probably easier to work with." :tutorial/id #uuid "00f219fa-5ae3-5e1d-acd0-7b7df752a433" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4351 :tutorial/title "Scaling the Vectors After the Gram-Schmidt Process"} :step/id #uuid "8e33ff42-ffc9-57ae-941b-8a447aded2e4" :step/math-academy-id 26458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134686}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134006}]}
